---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const GateForm = `Program: <name>
What it does: <two sentences>

Input:      <what you ask for, and how>
List:       <its name, what is in it, why the program needs it>
Procedure:  <its name, its parameters>
Inside it:  <the loop, and the decision>
Output:     <what comes out, and what it depends on>

Six sessions. What is done by the end of each one?
  46  the plan (this)
  47  ...
  48  ...
  49  ...   (requirement 4 gets checked this session)
  50  ...
  51  ...

What I will cut first if I run out of time:
  <name it now, while it is cheap>`;

const ReqFourShape = `function priceFor(tier)
{
  let total = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++)
  {
    if (tiers[i] === tier)
    {
      // ... add it up
    }
  }

  if (total === 0)
  {
    // ... nothing in that tier
  }

  return total;
}`;

const PprSegments = `PROCEDURE (i)   the procedure itself -- name, parameters,
                and an algorithm with sequencing, selection
                and iteration inside its body

PROCEDURE (ii)  where you call it

LIST (i)        where the data goes into the list

LIST (ii)       where that same list's data gets used`;

const Scale = `BUILD 1 was two sessions.
  A person could use it in under two minutes.

BUILD 2 is six.
  So it can be three times as big?

  No.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Build 2: The Long One</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- The Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Brief</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Six sessions of building.</b> You choose what to make &mdash;
            terminal or p5, your call. <b>100 points</b>, which is the largest
            single item in the quarter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Only two things are imposed, and they are the same two the Create Task
            will impose in Quarter 3: it must use a <b>list</b>, and a{" "}
            <b>student-developed procedure with a parameter that affects
            functionality</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Everything else is yours.</b> That is not generosity &mdash; it is
            the point. The Create Task hands you a blank page and eight sessions,
            and this is the last time you get to find out what you do with one
            while it still only costs points.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>There is a practice Personalized Project Reference, and it is
            homework due at session 52.</b> Four pasted code segments with{" "}
            <b>the comments stripped out</b>. More on that below, and read it
            before session 51 rather than on the night.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>No video.</b> That component is mechanical &mdash; one minute, no
            narration, show input and output &mdash; and it lands at session 64
            with the real thing. Nothing about it needs six sessions of rehearsal.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Build 2</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">The Long One</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Scoping Six Sessions</p>
              <p>All Six Requirements</p>
              <p>Coming Back Cold on Day Four</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>You Have Done This Once</h2>
              <p>Session 34: you wrote a plan. Session 35: you read it out loud.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Most of you said <i>"I had to cut..."</i></p>
              <p class="fragment">Today that memory is the most useful thing in the room.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Arithmetic Is a Trap</h2>
              <Code code={Scale} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Three Times the Sessions" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Six sessions does not buy you three times the program.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Because Day Four Is Not Like Day One</h2>
              <p>You come back to code you wrote three sessions ago.</p>
              <p class="fragment">You will find a bug you cannot reproduce.</p>
              <p class="fragment">You will decide halfway through that the structure is wrong.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Half of Build 2 is spent on Build 2.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which Is the Whole Reason This Block Is Long</h2>
              <p>Build 1 taught you scoping.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Build 2 teaches duration.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">They are different skills and one block cannot do both.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Requirements Imposed</h2>
              <p>A <b>list</b> the program actually needs.</p>
              <p class="fragment">A <b>procedure you wrote, with a parameter that changes what it does</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">That is all. The idea is yours.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>But Aim at All Six</h2>
              <p>The Create Task scores six requirements.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Four came free in Build 1. Two more are on the rubric here.</p>
              <p class="fragment">If you hit all six in Build 2, Quarter 3 is a repeat, not a first attempt.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And Number Four Again</h2>
              <p>Sequencing, selection, and iteration <b>inside your procedure's body</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">You cleared it once at Build 1.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>I am checking it again at session 49</b>, because people who got away with it assume they have it.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>If You Build in p5</h2>
              <p><code>setup()</code>, <code>draw()</code> and <code>mousePressed()</code> are <b>not yours</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The College Board excludes event handlers and main methods.</p>
              <p class="fragment">You need one alongside them that is. <b>Fifth time you have heard this.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today: The Gate</h2>
              <p><b>No code until your plan is approved.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">And your plan now includes a <b>session-by-session</b> breakdown.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Plus one line naming what you will cut first.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Name the Cut Now</h2>
              <p>Everyone cut something at Build 1.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Deciding what goes <i>while you are calm</i> is worth more than deciding at session 50.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And One New Thing</h2>
              <p>A <b>practice Personalized Project Reference</b>, due session 52.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Four code segments from your own program.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>With the comments taken out.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Read That Twice</h2>
              <p>The real Create Task submits the same code <b>two ways</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Program Code: <b>with</b> comments. Required.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Project Reference: <b>without</b> them. Comments there score you <b>zero on the whole task.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Session 52</h2>
              <p>Quarter checkpoint, exam format, 30 minutes.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Then a <b>gallery walk</b> &mdash; everyone runs their program, half the room circulates, then swap.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Scoping a six-session project against your own Build 1 experience</li>
                <li>Naming, in advance, what you will cut first</li>
                <li>Planning session by session rather than as one block of time</li>
                <li>Identifying which of your procedures satisfies the Create Task</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Personalized Project Reference</dt>
                  <dd>Four pasted code segments submitted with the Create Task, with no comments.</dd>
                  <dt>Gallery walk</dt>
                  <dd>Everyone runs their program; half the room circulates while half demonstrates, then swap.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Requirements -->
        <section id="requirements">
          <h2>All Six Requirements</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Two are imposed and all six are on the rubric.</b> These are the
            Create Task's own words, and they will not change in Quarter 3.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Input</b> &mdash; from the user, a device, an online data stream,
              or a file. <code>prompt()</code> in the terminal;{" "}
              <code>mousePressed()</code> in p5.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>At least one list</b> storing data that manages complexity and
              serves the program's purpose. <b>Imposed.</b> The test is still
              deletion &mdash; remove it and the program should break.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>At least one student-developed procedure</b> with a name, a return
              type if necessary, and <b>one or more parameters that have an effect
              on its functionality</b>. <b>Imposed.</b>
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>An algorithm with sequencing, selection, and iteration inside that
              procedure's body.</b> Checked at session 49.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Calls</b> to that procedure.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Output</b> based on the input and the functionality. Textual,
              visual, audible, or tactile.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>Requirements 5 and 6 arrive free if the rest are real.</b> They are
            on the rubric at 5 points each anyway, because a procedure that is never
            called is a procedure you did not need.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Requirement 4 -->
        <section id="requirement-four">
          <h2>Requirement 4, and Why It Gets Checked Twice</h2>

          <p>
            <b>This is the requirement that is satisfied program-wide and missed
            procedure-locally.</b> Build 1 drilled it and you cleared it. That is
            exactly why it is worth re-checking &mdash; a student who got away with
            it once assumes it is handled.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={ReqFourShape}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="All three, inside one procedure"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            Statements in order, a loop, and a decision &mdash; all between the
            braces of a procedure that takes a parameter. <b>Bodies left out on
            purpose.</b> Your program is yours.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>At session 49 I will ask you to put a finger on your procedure's
            opening and closing brace and name the three structures between
            them.</b> Session 49 is early enough to restructure. Session 51 is not.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Scoping -->
        <section id="scoping">
          <h2>How Big Is Six Sessions</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Build 1's rule was that a person could use the finished thing in
            under two minutes.</b> There is no equivalent rule here, because the
            constraint has changed shape.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Here is the honest guidance.</b> A six-session project is roughly a
            two-session project with:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>a second thing it can do, chosen by the user;</li>
            <li>data that persists across the whole run rather than one answer;</li>
            <li>output somebody would actually want to look at;</li>
            <li>and <b>three sessions of finding out what is wrong with it.</b></li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>That last line is not padding.</b> It is the median experience, and
            plans that do not budget for it are the plans that get cut in half at
            session 50.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">If you want a starting point</h3>

          <p>
            You do not have to use one of these. They are here so nobody spends
            session 46 staring at a blank page.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>A study planner.</b> Add subjects with a time cost, then ask it what
              fits in the hours you have tonight. This was the over-scoped example on
              the Build 1 brief &mdash; at six sessions it is about right.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>A quiz that keeps score across rounds</b> and tells you which
              category you are weakest in.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>A budget tracker.</b> Log spending against categories, then report by
              category with a warning when one is over.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>A team drafter.</b> Players with ratings, drafted into balanced
              teams, with the imbalance reported.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>p5: a drawing tool</b> where the mouse places shapes from a palette
              you can cycle, with an undo.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>p5: a data dashboard</b> for a dataset you care about, with more than
              one view of it. Lab 11 is the starting sketch.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Session 46 -->
        <section id="gate">
          <h2>Session 46: The Gate</h2>

          <p>
            <b>No code until your plan is approved.</b> Same rule as Build 1, and
            the plan is longer this time.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={GateForm}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="What to hand me"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Two parts of that are new and both matter more than the idea.</b>
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>The session-by-session breakdown.</b> Six lines. Not <i>"build the
              main part"</i> &mdash; something you could be held to. This is the
              first time you have had to plan duration rather than scope.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>The line naming what you will cut first.</b> Everybody cut
              something at Build 1. Deciding now, calmly, is worth more than
              deciding at session 50 with a broken program.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>Expect me to ask you about Build 1.</b> What did you drop, and what
            is different about this plan? A plan that ignores what happened last
            time is not a plan, it is optimism.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- The build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Sessions 47&ndash;51: The Build</h2>

          <p>
            Five sessions of work. Two things happen inside them that are not
            "keep going."
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Session 49 &mdash; the requirement 4 audit</h3>

          <p>
            Everyone gets checked. <b>Point at your procedure's braces and name the
            statement, the loop, and the <code>if</code> between them.</b> If one is
            missing you restructure that session, which is why the check is here and
            not at 51.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Every session &mdash; the day-four problem</h3>

          <p>
            You will come back to code you have not read for days.{" "}
            <b>Spend the first three minutes of each session reading what you wrote
            last time before you type anything.</b> It feels like waste. It is the
            cheapest three minutes in the block.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>And write down what you were in the middle of before you leave.</b>{" "}
            One line, in a comment. Future you has forgotten.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- The PPR -->
        <section id="ppr">
          <h2>The Practice Project Reference</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Homework, due at session 52.</b> Four code segments pasted from your
            own program.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={PprSegments}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="The four segments"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>And every comment taken out.</b>
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Why we are practicing this now</h3>

          <p>
            In Quarter 3 the Create Task submits your code <b>twice</b>. Component
            A is the full program <b>with comments</b> &mdash; comments are required
            there, for attribution. Component C is these four segments{" "}
            <b>with no comments at all</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Leaving comments in the Project Reference scores zero on the entire
            Create Task</b> &mdash; including the written responses you answer in
            May. It is 30% of your exam grade, lost to a copy-and-paste habit.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>There is a second way to score zero and it is a deadline.</b> If the
            Project Reference is not submitted as final on time, you sit the
            written-response section without it, answering four questions about your
            own program from memory.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Both of those are worth rehearsing on something that costs 15 points
            instead of 30% of an exam.</b> That is what this is.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Session 52 -->
        <section id="share-out">
          <h2>Session 52: Checkpoint, Then the Gallery Walk</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Quarter checkpoint first</b> &mdash; 30 minutes, exam format. Then 20
            minutes of share-out.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>It is a gallery walk, not presentations.</b> Everyone runs their
            program at their own machine. Half the room circulates while half
            demonstrates, then you swap. Twenty-five presentations do not fit in
            twenty minutes and most of the room stops watching by the sixth.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>When you are circulating, ask two questions at every machine:</b> what
            is the list for, and which procedure is the one that counts? You will
            answer those about your own program in Quarter 3, and hearing twenty-four
            other answers first is worth more than demonstrating once.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Rubric -->
        <section id="rubric">
          <h2>Rubric</h2>

          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>What</th>
                  <th class="text-end">Points</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Proposal approved at the session 46 gate, with a session-by-session plan and a named first cut</td>
                  <td class="text-end">10</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Input &mdash; the program asks for something and uses the answer</td>
                  <td class="text-end">5</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>A list the program actually needs</td>
                  <td class="text-end">10</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>A procedure you wrote, with a parameter that changes what it does</td>
                  <td class="text-end">15</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <b>Sequencing, selection, and iteration inside that procedure's body</b>
                  </td>
                  <td class="text-end">15</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Calls to that procedure</td>
                  <td class="text-end">5</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Output based on the input and the functionality</td>
                  <td class="text-end">5</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Sustained work across the block, including the session 49 audit</td>
                  <td class="text-end">10</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <b>Practice Project Reference &mdash; four segments, comments stripped, on time</b>
                  </td>
                  <td class="text-end">15</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Gallery walk &mdash; ran your program, answered both questions</td>
                  <td class="text-end">10</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="fw-bold">Total</td>
                  <td class="text-end fw-bold">100</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Same omission as Build 1:</b> nothing on this list scores how ambitious
            the idea was, or whether everything you planned got finished. A project
            that got cut to its named first cut and works loses nothing here.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Four things:</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Your <b>session 46 plan</b>, as approved on the day.</li>
            <li>Your <b>program</b>, with comments.</li>
            <li>
              Your <b>practice Project Reference</b> &mdash; four segments,{" "}
              <b>no comments</b>.
            </li>
            <li>
              One line naming <b>your procedure and its parameter</b>, so I know
              which one to check requirement 4 against.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>Items 2 and 3 contain the same code and must differ in exactly one
            way.</b> If you can hand those in correctly here, the Quarter 3 version
            is a formality.
          </p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Build Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
